### file-operations-s3 — example requests (IntelliJ IDEA HTTP Client)
#
# Start MinIO and the app first:  docker compose up -d  &&  ./mvnw spring-boot:run
# Run the requests top to bottom: responses save ids and URLs in variables for the later requests.
# Sample files are in ./samples. "fake-executable.pdf" is not a real program: only the "MZ" magic
# bytes of a Windows executable followed by zeros.
# MinIO console: http://localhost:9001 (minioadmin / minioadmin), bucket "files".

@host = http://localhost:8080

########## A. Upload through the API ##########

### A1. PUBLIC upload — 201, "publicUrl" is a permanent URL
POST {{host}}/api/files
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=boundary

--boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="logo.png"
Content-Type: image/png

< ./samples/logo.png
--boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="visibility"

PUBLIC
--boundary--

> {%
    client.test("created", () => client.assert(response.status === 201));
    client.global.set("publicFileId", response.body.id);
    client.global.set("publicUrl", response.body.publicUrl);
%}

### A2. Public URL — read straight from MinIO, no credentials (attachment, image/png)
GET {{publicUrl}}

### A3. PRIVATE upload — 201, no publicUrl
POST {{host}}/api/files
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=boundary

--boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="report.pdf"
Content-Type: application/pdf

< ./samples/report.pdf
--boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="visibility"

PRIVATE
--boundary--

> {% client.global.set("privateFileId", response.body.id); %}

### A4. Private object without a signature — 403 from MinIO
GET http://localhost:9000/files/private/{{privateFileId}}

> {% client.test("forbidden", () => client.assert(response.status === 403)); %}

### A5. Presigned download URL for the private file — valid for 5 minutes
GET {{host}}/api/files/{{privateFileId}}/download-url

> {% client.global.set("privateDownloadUrl", response.body.url); %}

### A6. Download with the presigned URL — straight from MinIO (attachment, application/pdf)
GET {{privateDownloadUrl}}

### A7. Download through the API instead
GET {{host}}/api/files/{{privateFileId}}/content

### A8. REJECTED: executable bytes named .pdf — 415, nothing reaches S3
POST {{host}}/api/files
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=boundary

--boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="invoice.pdf"
Content-Type: application/pdf

< ./samples/fake-executable.pdf
--boundary--

> {% client.test("rejected", () => client.assert(response.status === 415)); %}

########## B. Presigned upload: client -> S3 directly ##########

### B1. Ask for an upload URL — "size" must be the exact file size (report.pdf = 218 bytes)
POST {{host}}/api/files/uploads
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "filename": "report.pdf",
  "size": 218,
  "visibility": "PRIVATE"
}

> {%
    client.test("created", () => client.assert(response.status === 201));
    client.global.set("uploadId", response.body.id);
    client.global.set("uploadUrl", response.body.url);
%}

### B2. PUT the bytes straight to MinIO (pending/<id>); Content-Length is set from the file
PUT {{uploadUrl}}

< ./samples/report.pdf

> {% client.test("uploaded", () => client.assert(response.status === 200)); %}

### B3. Complete — the server validates the bytes: 200 READY
POST {{host}}/api/files/{{uploadId}}/complete

> {% client.test("ready", () => client.assert(response.body.status === "READY")); %}

### B4. Download the completed file
GET {{host}}/api/files/{{uploadId}}/content

### B5. REJECTED after upload: ask for a URL for "invoice.pdf" (fake-executable.pdf = 128 bytes)
POST {{host}}/api/files/uploads
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "filename": "invoice.pdf",
  "size": 128,
  "visibility": "PUBLIC"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("badUploadId", response.body.id);
    client.global.set("badUploadUrl", response.body.url);
%}

### B6. The PUT itself succeeds: S3 cannot check the content
PUT {{badUploadUrl}}

< ./samples/fake-executable.pdf

### B7. Complete — 415; the pending object is deleted and the file is REJECTED, never readable
POST {{host}}/api/files/{{badUploadId}}/complete

> {% client.test("rejected", () => client.assert(response.status === 415)); %}

### B8. Its status
GET {{host}}/api/files/{{badUploadId}}

### B9. Size mismatch: declare 999 bytes ...
POST {{host}}/api/files/uploads
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "filename": "notes.txt",
  "size": 999
}

> {% client.global.set("mismatchUploadUrl", response.body.url); %}

### B10. ... then PUT notes.txt (36 bytes) — 403 from MinIO: Content-Length is part of the signature
PUT {{mismatchUploadUrl}}

< ./samples/notes.txt

> {% client.test("refused by S3", () => client.assert(response.status === 403)); %}

### B11. Blocked extension — refused before any upload URL is issued (415)
POST {{host}}/api/files/uploads
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "filename": "setup.exe",
  "size": 128
}

### B12. Complete before uploading — 409
POST {{host}}/api/files/uploads
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "filename": "later.txt",
  "size": 10
}

> {% client.global.set("notUploadedId", response.body.id); %}

###
POST {{host}}/api/files/{{notUploadedId}}/complete

> {% client.test("conflict", () => client.assert(response.status === 409)); %}

########## C. Listing and deleting ##########

### C1. List — READY files only
GET {{host}}/api/files?page=0&size=20

### C2. Delete — 204; the object and its metadata are gone
DELETE {{host}}/api/files/{{publicFileId}}

### C3. The public URL no longer works — 404 from MinIO
GET {{publicUrl}}
